---
source: crates/shannon-tools/tests/tool_schema_snapshots.rs
expression: output
---
Agent: Spawn and manage AI agent teammates for collaborative problem-solving.

Delegate when a sub-task is self-contained and benefit from an isolated
context window: focused research, broad code exploration, or a specialist
review that would flood the lead's context. Sub-agents run in their own
session with their own tool registry (no recursive Agent tool) and return
only their final result — they never see this conversation. Restrict what
a sub-agent may do with `allowed_tools` (read-only exploration: Read/Grep/
Glob/Bash), `read_only: true` (read-only tools only), or `disallowed_tools`;
otherwise it inherits the parent's tool surface minus the parent's denylist.
`model` overrides the sub-agent's model id. Built-in agent types include
"oracle" (read-only senior staff reviewer — review/plan/analyze/advise),
"explorer", "planner", "code-reviewer". Operations: Spawn (run a task),
SendMessage (reply to a teammate), CreateTeam, Shutdown. Do NOT delegate
trivial single-tool lookups — just do them directly.
AnalyzeImage: Analyze an image file or URL. The image is sent to the LLM for visual analysis based on your prompt. Supports PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, BMP, ICO, and TIFF formats.
AnalyzeImages: Analyze multiple image files in ONE call (batch). Each image is sent to the LLM as part of a single vision request, with results organized under a '## <path>' heading per image. Accepts up to 20 paths; use this instead of calling AnalyzeImage repeatedly. Supports PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, BMP, ICO, and TIFF formats.
Bash: Executes a bash command and returns stdout/stderr.

Each call runs in a fresh shell in the working directory (no state
carries over; use `&&` to combine steps). Output is capped by the
harness — avoid commands that dump large files; use head/tail/grep
to scope output. A per-call `timeout` (ms) is supported: it defaults
to 120000 when omitted (override with SHANNON_BASH_TIMEOUT_MS) and
is hard-capped at 600000. Long-running or server processes should
use RunBackground and be polled with WaitForLog. When a sandbox is
active the command runs with restricted filesystem/network access —
the tool result reports denials. Sandboxing is applied by default
when a platform sandbox backend is available; set SHANNON_SANDBOX=off
to opt out.
Brief: Generate a concise task summary from conversation history or content
Config: Manage runtime configuration with get, set, list, delete, and reset operations
Cron: Schedule recurring or one-shot prompts for time-based task execution
DocsQuery: Fetch up-to-date documentation for libraries and frameworks. Resolves package names via docs.rs (Rust), npm (JavaScript/TypeScript), and PyPI (Python). Returns documentation snippets useful for writing correct code.
Edit: Performs exact string replacement in a file and returns the replacement
count, match locations and a unified diff.

You MUST Read the file first: `old_string` must match the current content
byte-for-byte (including whitespace/indentation) and be UNIQUE in the file
unless `replace_all` is true — include enough surrounding lines to make a
short snippet unique. A missing or ambiguous match fails with the nearest
candidates shown. Fallback: when old_string is not in the current content
but IS in the file's git HEAD version (the file moved under you), the edit
is applied via a three-way merge; textual conflicts are returned in the
result instead of being silently overwritten. Use MultiEdit for batched
edits and Write only to replace a whole file.
GitBranch: List, create, switch, and delete git branches
GitDiff: Show git diffs with support for staged, unstaged, and commit range diffs
GitLog: View git commit history with filtering and formatting options
GitSafety: Check if a git command is safe before executing it
GitStash: Manage git stashes: list, push, pop, drop, and apply stashed changes
Glob: Fast file-pattern matching tool that works with any codebase size.

Patterns are matched against paths RELATIVE to the search directory (so
`*.rs` matches only top-level files; use `**/*.rs` to recurse) and
gitignore-aware traversal skips ignored paths like `target/`. Results are
sorted by modification time (most recent first) with size/mtime metadata,
and capped at 100 — when a broad pattern is truncated the result says so,
so prefer a narrower pattern or a subdirectory `path` over enumerating
everything. Escaping patterns (`..`, absolute) are rejected.
Grep: Searches file contents with regex patterns across a directory tree.

Returns matches grouped per file. Output modes: `content` (matching lines
with optional context_before/context_after lines and line numbers),
`files` (matching paths only — prefer this when you just need to locate
code), and `count` (matches per file). Results are capped at `max_results`
(default 1000); gitignored paths are skipped. Prefer Glob for finding files
by name and Read for reading a whole file — Grep is for locating patterns
inside file contents. Supports include/exclude globs and
case_insensitive matching.
KillBackground: Terminate a background child by name, wait for it to exit (bounded), and return its exit code plus the captured tail of stdout/stderr.
McpAuth: Manage OAuth authentication for MCP servers that require it
McpResource: Read and list resources from MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers
MergeResolve: Resolve merge conflict markers in a file by choosing 'ours' or 'theirs' for each conflict
MultiEdit: Apply multiple file edits atomically — all edits succeed or none are applied
NotebookEdit: Edit Jupyter notebook (.ipynb) cells - replace, insert, or delete cells
PowerShell: Executes PowerShell commands and returns output
REPL: Execute a REPL command that wraps primitive tool calls for batch operations
Read: Read a file from the filesystem.

Output lines are numbered cat -n style ("{line_number}\t{content}"); cite
locations as file_path:line. Returns up to 2000 lines per call; large files
are truncated with a notice — use `offset`/`limit` to page through them (the
line numbers stay absolute to the file). Images (png/jpg/gif/webp) are
returned as attachments the model can view. Binary files are reported as
such instead of being dumped as mojibake. You MUST read a file before editing
it — Edit requires that old_string matches the file exactly, which you can
only know from a fresh Read.
RunBackground: Spawn a long-running bash command and stream stdout/stderr into a line-bounded ring buffer keyed by `name`. Re-calling with the same `name` kills the previous child first. Pair with `WaitForLog` to block until a regex/substring appears, and `KillBackground` to shut down.
ScheduleWakeup: Schedule a prompt to be enqueued after a delay (for /loop dynamic pacing). Call with delaySeconds and prompt to schedule; omit or call without arguments to end the loop. Cache optimization: delays <= 270s keep the prompt cache warm; 270-300s risks cache expiry; > 300s pays cold read cost. Prefer 270s for active work, 600s+ for idle waits.
SendMessage: Send messages to agent teammates for collaborative problem-solving
Skill: Invoke user-callable slash-command skills for specialized workflows
Sleep: Wait for a specified duration without holding a shell process
StructuredOutput: Return structured output in the requested format. Call this tool exactly once at the end of your response to provide the structured output.
TaskCreate: Create a new task with subject, description, and optional metadata
TaskGet: Get details of a specific task by ID
TaskList: List all tasks with optional status filter
TaskOutput: Get the output/result of a task execution by its ID
TaskStop: Stop or cancel a running task by its ID
TaskUpdate: Update an existing task's status, subject, or description
TeamDelete: Remove a team and clean up its associated task list
TodoWrite: Create and manage a structured task checklist for the current session.

Use it for multi-step work — 3 or more steps, or any task complex enough
that progress could be lost track of: write the plan as items up front,
keep exactly one item in_progress while working, and mark items completed
as soon as they finish. Each call REPLACES the whole list, so always send
the full updated set; when every item is completed the list clears. Skip it for single trivial actions that need no tracking.
WaitForLog: Poll a background process's stdout/stderr ring buffer until a substring (match_kind=is) or regex (match_kind=re) matches, the process exits, or the timeout elapses. Returns immediately on match or exit.
WebFetch: Fetches a URL from the internet and optionally extracts its contents
WebSearch: Search the web for information using a real search API
Worktree: Manage git worktree sessions for isolated branch work
Write: Writes `content` to a file, overwriting it entirely if it exists and
creating it (with any missing parent directories) if it does not.

Prefer Edit for targeted changes to an existing file — Write replaces the
whole content and silently discards anything not included, so re-read the
file first if you only saw part of it. The write is atomic (temp file +
rename): a failure never leaves a partial file. Results report the byte
count written; content over 10 MB is rejected.
app_open: Open a file, folder, URL, or application with the OS default handler (Windows ShellExecuteW / macOS open / Linux xdg-open).
applescript: Run AppleScript or JXA against scriptable macOS applications (Mail, Calendar, Reminders, Messages, Finder, Notes, …), or run a Shortcuts shortcut. Requires macOS; the first call per application triggers Apple's standard Automation permission prompt.
ask_user_question: Ask the user a question during execution. Supports single-select, multi-select, and free-text input. Use this when you need confirmation, option selection, or information from the user.
auto_commit: Smart git commit tool. Stages changes (all or specific files), generates semantic commit messages from diffs, and creates commits. Supports dry-run preview and co-author trailers. Shows git status and diff stats before committing.
browser_click: Click on the given tab. Prefer `ref` (an element id like "e3" from browser_snapshot) — real mouse events fire at the element's center. Fall back to viewport coordinates (x, y) when no snapshot exists.
browser_close: Close a browser tab. Use `"all": true` to close every tab (the Chrome process keeps running).
browser_console: Return the buffered console messages (log/debug/info/error/warning) for the given tab, oldest first. Messages are captured from the moment the tab was opened; the buffer holds the most recent 500 entries.
browser_evaluate: Run a JavaScript expression in the page and return its value (strings verbatim, other values as JSON). Full DOM access — use for data extraction or UI state the dedicated tools can't reach.
browser_fill: Set the value of an input/textarea/contenteditable element by ref (from browser_snapshot). Fires input+change events so reactive frameworks see the value.
browser_navigate: Open a URL in a new browser tab. Returns the new tab_id for subsequent actions. Requires the `local-browser` feature.
browser_press_key: Press a key on the given tab (e.g. "Enter", "Escape", "Tab", "ArrowDown", "a", "F5"). Goes to the focused element.
browser_screenshot: Capture a PNG screenshot of the given tab (viewport or full page). PNG bytes are returned in tool-result metadata under `image/png`.
browser_scroll: Scroll the page by `amount` pixels (default 600). direction: "down" (default) or "up".
browser_snapshot: Return the page's title, URL, and an index of visible interactive elements (links, buttons, inputs, …) with element refs usable by browser_click/browser_fill.
browser_tabs: List the open browser tabs in this Shannon process.
browser_text: Return the page's full text content (title + URL + innerText, ≤8KB) for reading articles/documentation. For interacting with controls use browser_snapshot instead.
browser_type: Type text into the focused field on the given tab.
clipboard_read: Read the current text (CF_UNICODETEXT) from the system clipboard. May contain secrets the user copied — never echo it verbatim unless asked.
clipboard_write: Write text to the system clipboard (CF_UNICODETEXT), replacing whatever was there.
code_actions: Get available code actions (quick fixes, refactorings) for a range in a file. Uses the language server to suggest fixes for diagnostics and available refactorings.
computer: Interact with the computer desktop: take screenshots, click, type, scroll, and press keys. Coordinates are in [0-1024, 0-768] range and scaled to actual screen resolution.
document_symbol: List all symbols (functions, classes, methods, variables, etc.) in a file. Uses the language server to provide a hierarchical view of the document's symbol structure.
enter_plan_mode: Enter plan mode for read-only analysis and planning. File modifications will be disabled.
exit_plan_mode: Exit plan mode and return to normal editing mode.
find_references: Find all references to the symbol at a given position in a file. Uses the language server to locate all usages of a symbol across the codebase.
get_mcp_prompt: Get a prompt from an MCP (Model Context Protocol) server. Provide server_name, prompt_name, and optional arguments to retrieve the prompt messages.
get_plan_status: Get the current plan mode status, including active state and any plan content.
gh_issue_list: List GitHub issues for the current repository
gh_issue_view: View a specific GitHub issue with comments
gh_pr_create: Create a pull request on GitHub
gh_pr_list: List pull requests for the current repository
gh_pr_view: View a specific pull request with optional diff
go_to_definition: Find the definition of the symbol at a given position in a file. Uses the language server (e.g., rust-analyzer) to locate where a symbol is defined.
hover: Get type information and documentation for the symbol at a given position. Uses the language server to provide hover information such as type signatures, doc comments, and other contextual information.
list_mcp_prompts: List available prompts from MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers. Returns prompt names, descriptions, and argument schemas. Optionally filter by server_name.
list_mcp_resources: List available resources from MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers. Returns resource URIs, names, descriptions, and MIME types. Optionally filter by server_name.
read_mcp_resource: Read the content of a specific MCP (Model Context Protocol) resource by providing the server name and resource URI. Returns the resource content as text.
rename_symbol: Rename a symbol (variable, function, class, etc.) across the entire codebase. Uses the language server to find all occurrences and compute the correct edits for renaming.
window_focus: Bring a window to the foreground by title or process substring. Restores it if minimized.
window_list: List visible top-level windows: title, owning process, and handle id. Use the title with `window_focus` or `computer` ui actions.
workspace_symbol: Search for symbols across the entire workspace by name. Uses the language server to find functions, classes, structs, etc. matching a query pattern across all files in the project.
